    <p>In the previous unit, we learned how to draw squares with loops. Let's put that code in a function, so 
      that it can be called multiple times to draw multiple squares. First, try it with a mixed up code problem, and
      then write the code yourself.
    </p>

    <activity label="turtleFunctionMixedUp" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following code should define a function that draws a square using a turtle called tina, 
          but it is mixed up. 
          Drag the needed code to the right side in the correct order.
          Remember that the statements in the function must be indented! To
          indent a block drag it further right.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <blocks>
        <block order="3">
          <cline>def drawSquare():</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>    for i in range(4):</cline>
          </block>
        <block order="2">
          <cline>        tina.forward(50)</cline>
          <cline>        tina.right(90)</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
      </activity>

   <activity label="turtleSquareFunction">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a function called <c>drawSquare()</c> with no parameters that draws a square of size 50 using the turtle. 
          Move the loop into this function and then call the function to draw the square multiple times.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
import turtle
tina = turtle.Turtle()
tina.shape("turtle")
tina.color("green")

# Put this code in a def drawSquare() function (no parameters)
# Make sure you indent the code inside the function 
#  and keep the indent inside the loop
for count in range(4):
    tina.forward(50)
    tina.right(90)

# Add a function call to drawSquare() here

tina.goto(-100,150) # move turtle up
# Replace this code with a function call to drawSquare()
for count in range(4):
    tina.forward(50)
    tina.right(90)

# Move the turtle to a new location and call drawSquare() again

          </code>
          <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        # remove comments
        actual = "\n".join(
                       line for line in self.getEditorText().splitlines()
                      if line.strip() and not line.lstrip().startswith("#")
                      )

        self.assertIn("def drawSquare():",self.getEditorText(), "Define a function called drawSquare()")
        for_count = actual.count("for ")
        self.assertEqual(1, for_count, "Use just 1 for loop")
        drawSquare_count = actual.count("drawSquare()")
        self.assertLessEqual(3, drawSquare_count, "Call drawSquare() at least 3 times")

myTests().main()
          </tests>
          </program>
    </activity>

  </subsection>
 
 <subsection xml:id="arguments">
    <title>Arguments and Parameters</title>

    <idx>arguments</idx>
    <idx>parameters</idx>

     <note>
      <title>3.2.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Procedures can use parameters to receive input. A <term>parameter</term> is a variable name
        listed in the procedure definition. An <term>argument</term> is the actual value provided
        when the procedure is called.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
  Let’s take another look at the "This Old Man" song and see if we can
  replace more repeated code. Each verse of the song is similar except it uses a different number and rhyme.
  If we can pull these out into variables, we can write a single function that can print any verse of the song!
  Click on the words that are different in the lines that are repeated to
  discover what variables we need to add to the functions.
</p>

<activity label="this-old-man-find-different-words">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on the words that are different in the repeated lines.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Look for lines that are similar except for a different number or action
      and click on those words.
    </p>
  </feedback>

  <areas>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played</area> <area correct="yes">one</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my</area> <area correct="yes">thumb</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played</area> <area correct="yes">two</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my</area> <area correct="yes">shoe</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
  <cline><area correct="no">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
  </areas>
</activity>



<p>
  We can make functions even more powerful and more abstract by giving them
  parameters for the data that they need to do their job. A
  <term>parameter</term> is a variable listed in the definition of a function
  and can be used inside the function body. This allows values, called
  <term>arguments</term>, to be passed into the function. The argument value is copied into the 
  corresponding parameter variable. Many people use the terms "parameter" and "argument" interchangeably, but formally, 
  the parameter is the variable in the function definition and the argument is the value passed to the function when it is called. 
</p>
<p>
  For example, here is a procedure called <c>forward</c> with a parameter variable called <c>pixels</c> that we used with turtles 
  with procedure calls like <c>forward(50)</c> telling it to move forward 40 or 100 pixels. The value 40 is the argument that is passed to the parameter variable <c>pixels</c> in the procedure definition.
</p>

  <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-defn-call.png" alt="Parameter and Argument" width="90%"/>

<p>Going back to our song, we can make a function called <c>verse</c> that takes the number and the rhyme as parameters and uses them to 
  print any verse! The parameter variables <c>number</c> and <c>rhyme</c> will hold different values each time the function is called. </p>

<program language="python"> 
  <code> 
# This function prints a verse for a given number and rhyme. 
# @param number - the number used in the verse 
# @param rhyme - the word that rhymes with the number

def verse(number, rhyme):
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()
</code>
</program>

<p>The <c>verse</c> function can now be used to print any verse of the song. The values passed to the function become the values of 
  the parameter variables. The main part of the program can be just calls to the <c>verse</c> function. Main program code often 
  looks like an outline for the program, calling all the functions to do the work. </p>

<program language="python"> 
  <code> 
verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
</code>
</program>

<p>Notice that the same function is called two times with different arguments. When <c>verse("one", "thumb")</c> is called, the 
  parameter <c>number</c> gets the value <c>"one"</c> and the parameter <c>rhyme</c> gets the value <c>"thumb"</c>. When 
  <c>verse("two", "shoe")</c> is called, the same parameters receive different values. The function does not need to change. The 
  arguments provide the values that makes each verse different. </p>

  <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-verse-call.png" alt="Calling verse(number, rhyme)" width="70%"/>


<activity label="codelensSong2"> 
  <statement> 
    <p> Click on the next button below the code to step through the code. Watch the red arrow jump to the <c>verse</c> function each time 
      it is called. Pay attention to how the arguments are passed into the parameter variables. 
    </p> </statement>

<program interactive="codelens" language="python" label="codeLens3" xml:id="codelensid3"> 
  <code>
def chorus(): 
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")

def verse(number, rhyme): 
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()

verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
</code>
</program>
</activity>

<p> Let’s try adding more verses to the song. With the power of procedural abstraction using a function with parameters, 
  it becomes very easy to add more verses to the song, just by calling the <c>verse</c> function with different arguments.
  Parameters make it more general, flexible, and reusable! 
 </p>

<activity label="ThisOldManSong2"> 
  <statement> 
    <p>Run the following code to see the song "This Old Man" using the <c>verse</c> function. 
  Add another verse using <c>"three"</c> and the rhyme <c>"knee"</c> and another verse using <c>"four"</c> and the rhyme <c>"door"</c> by
  calling the <c>verse</c> function with the two arguments. 
</p>

</statement>

<program interactive="activecode" language="python"> 
  <code> 
def chorus(): 
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")
    print()  # print extra empty line

def verse(number, rhyme): 
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()

verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
# TODO:
# Call verse again with "three" and "knee"
# Call verse again with "four" and "door"


</code>

<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
       
        self.assertIn("verse(\"three\",\"knee\")", code, "Call verse() with \"three\" and \"knee\"")
        self.assertIn("verse(\"four\",\"door\")", code, "Call verse() with \"four\" and \"door\"")

myTests().main()
</tests>

</program> 
</activity>

<p>Let's add one more abstraction! Let's add <term>data abstraction</term> using lists along with <term>procedural abstraction</term>.
We can create two parallel lists of the <c>number</c> and <c>rhyme</c> values and iterate through them. This abstraction
makes our code even more flexible and makes it even easier to add another verse by just adding a new element to each list.</p>

<program language="python">
  <code>
numbers = ["one", "two", "three"]
rhymes = ["thumb", "shoe", "knee"]
# Iterate through the lists and call verse() for each number and rhyme
for i in range(len(numbers)):
    verse(numbers[i], rhymes[i])
  </code>
</program>

<activity label="ThisOldManSongLists"> 
  <statement> 
    <p>Change the code below to use two parallel lists of the <c>number</c> and <c>rhyme</c> values and iterate through them to call the <c>verse</c> function for each number and rhyme. For help, see the code above. Create your list with 3 elements to start with and
      then extend it to 4 or 5 elements to see how easy it is to add more verses to the song using lists and a for loop.
</p>

</statement>

<program interactive="activecode" language="python"> 
  <code> 
def chorus(): 
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")
    print()

def verse(number, rhyme): 
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()

# Create 2 parallel lists: numbers with the values "one", "two", "three", "four"
#     and rhymes with the values "thumb", "shoe", "knee", "door"

# Use a for loop to iterate through the lists and call verse() for each number and rhyme in the lists
# instead of the 2 lines below
verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
</code>

<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
       
        self.assertIn('["one","two","three","four"', code, "Create a list of at least 4 numbers")
        self.assertIn('["thumb","shoe","knee","door"', code, "Create a list of at least 4 rhymes")
        self.assertIn("for i in range", self.getEditorText(), "Use for i in range loop"")
        self.assertIn("verse(numbers[i],rhymes[i])", code, "Call verse(numbers[i], rhymes[i]) in the loop")

myTests().main()
</tests>

</program> 
</activity>
  
    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure may not need parameters when it always performs the same steps with
        no changing input (for example, displaying an instructions screen or resetting a
        game to a default starting state). Procedures need parameters when they need
        input information to complete the intended task. If a procedure should work for
        many different inputs (for example, greeting different users or calculating totals
        for different numbers), parameters make it flexible and reusable because the
        program can pass different arguments each time it is called. Using parameters
        when input varies helps avoid duplicating code and keeps the program easier to
        update.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Procedures with parameters make it easier to adapt an app's behavior or output
        to different users and situations without rewriting code. A single procedure with
        parameters can generate many clear, personalized messages for different users by
        using different argument values each time it is called.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>Let's now write our own functions with parameters. Let's write a procedure or function that welcomes a user.
      In AP CSP pseudocode, we would start the procedure definition with the header <c>procedure welcome(name)</c> where the parameter variable is put inside the parentheses after the procedure name. Then, we can call <c>welcome</c> with different values for the parameter variable <c>name</c> to print a personalized welcome message. 
    </p>

   <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-welcome.png" alt="Parameter and Argument" width="50%"/>
 
    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.6</title>
      <p>
        A procedure is defined with a name and a list of zero or more parameters. When
        the following code runs, a procedure named <c>pName</c> is defined with zero or
        more parameters (<c>p1, p2, ...</c>). When the procedure is called, the statements
        in <c>&lt;block of statements&gt;</c> execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
procedure pName(p1, p2, ...)
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}</pre>
    </note>

        <p>In AP CSP pseudocode, a procedure with parameters is defined using the following syntax:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
procedure procedureName(param1, param2, ...)
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}
</code>
    </program>
 
    <p>In Python, a function with parameters is defined using the following syntax:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
def functionName(param1, param2, ...):
    # function body
</code>
    </program>


    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.3</title>
      <p>
        In a procedure call, the values inside the parentheses are <term>arguments</term>. When the
        call runs, each argument value is assigned to the matching parameter in the
        procedure definition. This allows the same procedure to run with different input
        values. When the following code runs, <c>a1</c> is assigned to the first parameter
        and <c>a2</c> is assigned to the second parameter before the procedure's statements
        execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
pName(a1, a2)</pre>
    </note>

    <p>Python and AP CSP pseudocode have very similar simple procedure or function call with the procedure name
      followed by the argument values inside the parentheses. The number of arguments in a call must match the number of parameters in the 
      procedure definition. Each argument value is assigned to the matching parameter in the procedure definition in order.
    </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
# Python and AP CSP pseudocode procedure/function call
functionName(arg1, arg2, ...)
    </code>
    </program>

    <activity label="welcome-function">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a procedure called <c>welcome</c> that takes a parameter for the user's
          name and prints Hello using the name. Then call the procedure with your name as
          the argument.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          # TODO: Write the welcome function using def and a parameter name
          #       Inside the function, print a Hello and the name


          # Function Calls
          welcome("World")
          # TODO: Call welcome with your name as the argument
          # TODO: Call welcome again with a different name as the argument

        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
        self.assertIn("def", code, "Define the welcome function with def")
        self.assertIn("welcome(name)", code, "Define the welcome function with a parameter name")
        self.assertIn("World", self.getOutput(), "The output should include a welcome message to World")
        count = code.count("welcome(")
        self.assertLessEqual(4, count, "Call welcome() at least 2 more times")

myTests().main()
</tests>
</program>
    </activity>

    <p>Let's also add parameters to our turtle square function so that we can draw squares of different sizes and colors.
    </p>

   <activity label="turtleSquareFunctionParams">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a function called <c>drawSquare(size, color)</c> that draws a square of the specified size and color using the turtle. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
import turtle
tina = turtle.Turtle()
tina.shape("turtle")
tina.color("green")

# TODO: Change this function to take 2 parameters for size and color
def drawSquare():
    # TODO: Change the code below to use the color and size variables
    #   instead of "green" and 50
    tina.begin_fill()
    tina.color("green")
    for count in range(4):
        tina.forward(50)
        tina.right(90)
    tina.end_fill()

# TODO: Add arguments of 150 and "red" to the function call below
drawSquare()

tina.goto(-100,150) # move turtle up
# TODO: Add arguments of 100 and "blue" to the function call below
drawSquare()

# Move the turtle to a new location and call drawSquare with any arguments

          </code>
          <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
        self.assertIn("drawSquare(size,color):", code, "Add parameters for size and color to the drawSquare function")
        self.assertIn("tina.color(color)", code, "Use the color parameter in the drawSquare function")
        self.assertIn("tina.forward(size)", code, "Use the size parameter in the drawSquare function")
        self.assertIn("drawSquare(150,\"red\")", code, "Call drawSquare(150,\"red\")")
        self.assertIn("drawSquare(100,\"blue\")", code, "Call drawSquare(100,\"blue\")")

myTests().main()
          </tests>
          </program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure can also be called with no arguments (empty parentheses) when it has
        no parameters. The number of arguments in a call must match the number of
        parameters in the procedure definition.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.5</title>
      <p>
        To determine the result of a procedure call, programmers trace the call by
        matching arguments to parameters, stepping through the procedure's statements in
        order, tracking any variable updates and outputs, and then returning to where the
        procedure was called to continue program execution.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>When tracing through code that has procedures/functions, it is important to write down and keep track of the 
      parameter variables and their values with each function call. Remember to match the arguments with the parameter in order.
    </p>
    
  <activity label="parametersTrace">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the following pseudocode for a procedure <c>song</c>.
    </p>
<program language="python">
  <code>
procedure song(food1, food2)
{
    print("I like to eat, eat, eat", food1, "and", food2, "!")
}
</code>
</program>
<p>Given the following procedure calls, what would be printed when the code is run? </p>
  <program language="python">
    <code>
song("bananas", "apples")
song("pizza", "pineapples") 
</code> </program>
 </statement>
  <choices randomize="yes">
<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><pre>I like to eat, eat, eat apples and bananas!</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      The first procedure call is <c>song("bananas", "apples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"bananas"</c>, is assigned to the
      <c>food1</c> parameter.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice correct="yes">
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat bananas and apples!
I like to eat, eat, eat pizza and pineapples!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Correct! The first procedure call is <c>song("bananas", "apples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"bananas"</c>, is assigned to the
      <c>food1</c> parameter and the second argument, <c>"apples"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food2</c> parameter. The second procedure call is <c>song("pizza", "pineapples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"pizza"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food1</c> parameter and the second argument, <c>"pineapples"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food2</c> parameter.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat apples and bananas!
I like to eat, eat, eat pineapples and pizzas!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Remember that arguments are assigned to parameters in order. The
      first argument is assigned to the first parameter variable, etc.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat pizzas and pineapples!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Both procedure calls will print a line, so there should be two lines printed. 
    </p>
    </feedback>
</choice>
  </choices>
</activity>


    </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="event-handlers">
    <title>Event-Handling Functions and Global Variables</title>

    <idx>event handling</idx>
    <idx>global variables</idx>
 

    <p>
      Event-handling functions are called when an event occurs in a graphical user interface (GUI), 
      for example when a button is clicked or a key is pressed. Python turtles can respond to mouse clicks 
      and keyboard presses with some fun event-handlers called <c>onscreenclick</c> and <c>onkey</c> that
      call a specified function like <c>myFunction</c> when a mouse or key press event occurs. This is the only place
      where () are not used after a function name in Python.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
# Call myFunction when the mouse is clicked on the screen
# myFunction must take 2 parameters for the x and y coordinates of the click
turtle.onscreenclick(myFunction)  

# Call myFunction when the "Up" arrow key is pressed
turtle.onkey(myFunction, "Up")
turtle.listen()  # Start listening for key presses

# Call myFunction after 500 milliseconds (reduce to make faster)
turtle.ontimer(myFunction, 800)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>If we want the turtle to turn with the arrows on the keyboard, we need to use the <c>setheading</c> function 
      which turns the turtle to a specific north, south, east, or west direction. Since the Python turtles always start
      facing east or to the right of the screen, that is set to <c>setheading(0)</c> for 0 degrees. From their starting position, the turtle can be turned to face 
      north/up with <c>setheading(90)</c>, west/left with <c>setheading(180)</c>, and south/down with <c>setheading(270)</c>. 
      In the code below, event handling functions have been written for the mouse click and the up and right arrow keys. 
      Try adding the event handling functions for the down and left arrow keys to move the turtle in those directions.
    </p>
 
    <activity label="turtleEventHandlers">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the event-handling functions below to move the turtle around the screen with mouse clicks and arrow keys.
          Note that when you click on Run in Runestone, the turtle window will only respond for 20 seconds. You will
          need to click inside the turtle window to give it focus before pressing the arrow keys. </p>
       <p>
          Change the onclick_handler to change the turtle's color. Change it back when the up arrow is pressed.
          Add the functions for the "Down" and "Left" arrow keys using the <c>setheading</c> function with 270 and 180 degrees.
          Draw something fun with the turtle and the keyboard arrow keys!
        </p>
      </statement>
<program interactive="activecode" language="python">
  <code>
import turtle

t = turtle.Turtle()
t.shape("turtle")

# Try clicking on the screen away from the turtle to move it to a new location
# This will work for 20 seconds.
def click_handler(x, y):
    t.goto(x, y)
    # 1. TODO: change the turtle color

# This will call the click_handler function when the mouse is clicked on the screen
turtle.onscreenclick(click_handler)

# Define movement functions
def move_right():
    t.setheading(0)    # Face straight right
    t.forward(20)

def move_up():
    t.setheading(90)   # Face straight up
    t.forward(20)
    # 2. TODO: Add code to change the turtle color back 


# 3. TODO: Define move_left (180 degrees) and move_down (270 degrees) functions 



turtle.onkey(move_right, "Right")    
turtle.onkey(move_up, "Up")
# 4. TODO: Add onkey calls for move_left and move_down functions


turtle.listen()    
  </code>  
  <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("def move_down():",self.getEditorText(), "Define a function called move_down()")
        self.assertIn("def move_left():",self.getEditorText(), "Define a function called move_left()")
        self.assertIn("t.color",self.getEditorText(), "Change the color of the turtle")
        self.assertIn('turtle.onkey(move_left,"Left")',self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""), "Add an onkey call for move_left()")
        self.assertIn('turtle.onkey(move_down,"Down")',self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""), "Add an onkey call for move_down()")

myTests().main()
  </tests>    
</program>
</activity>

  <p><term>Local variables</term> are variables that are defined inside a function and can only be used within that function. 
  <term>Global variables</term> are variables that are defined outside of any function and can be used anywhere in the program.
  In Python, if you change a variable using assignment (<c>=</c>) inside a function, it becomes a local variable. If you want to refer to 
  a global variable, you need to declare that variable as <c>global</c> before using it, usually in the first line of the function definition. 
  Another option is to pass and return the varialble as a parameter to the function. 
  </p>

  <program language="python">
      <code>
# Global variable
score = 0

def increase_score():
    global score       # Declare that we are using the global variable
    amount = 5         # amount is a local variable
    score += amount    # Increase the global score by amount
</code>
</program>

<activity label="globalVariable">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Try running the code below to see the error where score is assumed to be a local variable,
      and the global variable score is not changed. 
      Add the line <c>global score</c> in the first line of the <c>increase_score()</c> function to fix the error. 
    </p>
  </statement>
  <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
    <code>
# Global variable
score = 0

# TODO: Run to see that score is not changing. 
# Declare score as global in the function to fix the error.
def increase_score():
    amount = 10         # amount is a local variable
    score = amount      # Change score to amount 
      
print("Score before increase:", score)
increase_score()
print("Score after increase:", score)
increase_score()
print("Score after increase:", score)
    </code>
    <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("global score",self.getEditorText(), "Add global score in the first line of the increase_score() function")
        self.assertIn("10",self.getOutput(), "Score should be 10 after the first call to increase_score()")
       
myTests().main()
    </tests>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>Let's create a whack-a-mole game where the turtle randomly moves on the screen, and the user tries to catch it
      with mouse clicks. This game will use a global score variable.  </p>
  
